<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Can you use both?</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Yes, and many businesses do. A common pattern is Google Ads to catch people searching, and Facebook or Instagram adverts shown to people who visited your website and did not buy. Start with one, get it paying for itself, then add the other.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Common mistakes</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Sending every click to the home page.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Choosing broad search terms that attract people looking for jobs or free advice.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Boosting posts and calling it advertising.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Changing everything after three days, before there is enough data to judge.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Never checking which searches or audiences actually produced customers.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

